effective. Whereas the patient’s stiffness and opsoclo-
nus responded to PE temporarily, neuropsychiatric
symptoms due to progressive encephalopathy only
partially improved. This is not surprising in view of
the widespread CNS inflammation, which would not
be expected to be rapidly altered by removing autoanti-
bodies from the circulation. Still, as antibody binding
and T cell infiltration occurred in the same regions of
the CNS, it is conceivable that autoantibodies upon
binding could themselves induce encephalopathy and
promote cell-mediated toxicity.
In a few antibody-associated paraneoplastic disor-
ders, removal of the antibody leads to clinical im-
provement, including the Lambert-Eaton myasthenic
syndrome and myasthenia gravis with malignant
thymoma.
10 In most paraneoplastic disorders, plas-
mapheresis and antibody removal are ineffective.
Here, the pathogenic role of autoantibodies is sug-
gested by immunocytochemical findings and by the
temporary clinical response to antibody removal.
Passive transfer studies are underway to confirm the
causal role of autoantibodies in this syndrome.
